    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. When you mention \"localized public safety reports\" and \"social strain,\" how do we actually weight those in a model? Specifically, when we see these rapid shifts in ethnic composition or a sudden increase in non-native speaking populations, does history or existing actuarial data suggest a higher correlation with increased volatility or higher crime risk compared to more homogeneous areas? I just want to understand if we should be building in a higher risk premium for these specific demographic transitions.",
